- Requirement: Identify gaps in course information 
- Prompt V1: You are a product marketing manager, review the notes from the course discovery session with university X and identify the gaps in information based on what should make up a complete product brief
====
Response: 
1. Program Details
•	Missing specifics on course structure and content:
•	Detailed course/module list with credit points or hours per subject.
•	Breakdown of foundational vs. elective subjects.
•	Delivery format specifics (online, face-to-face, hybrid).
•	Duration and schedule details (semester/trimester system).
•	Missing assessment methods:
•	Types of assessments used (exams, projects, presentations, practicals).
•	Weighting of assessments.
2. Market Analysis
•	Competitive landscape:
•	Specific competitor programs for benchmarking.
•	Unique selling points compared to competitors beyond general statements.
•	Market demand and trends:
•	Data or insights on demand for graduates in AI, data science, and emerging tech.
•	Labor market needs analysis supporting program relevance.

3. Target Audience
•	More granular audience segmentation:
•	Demographic and psychographic profiles.
•	Geographic reach (local, national, international students).
•	Specific needs or pain points of target groups.
•	Marketing personas:
•	Detailed personas for marketing communications and positioning.
4. Admissions
•	Entry requirements details:
•	Specific academic prerequisites (e.g., minimum ATAR or equivalent).
•	English language proficiency requirements.
•	Any prerequisite subjects or experience.
•	Application process and timelines.
•	Scholarships, financial aid, and fees:
•	Program costs, tuition fees, and additional expenses.
•	Available scholarships or financial support options.
5. Industry Partnerships
•	Names and profiles of industry partners:
•	Specific companies or organisations involved.
•	Nature and extent of partnerships (internships, guest lectures, joint research).
•	Certification details:
•	Which certifications align with the course content.
•	Whether certifications are included or optional.
•	Process and support for students pursuing certification.

6. Career Outcomes
•	Graduate employment data:
•	Placement rates, average salaries, and types of roles graduates secure.
•	Alumni success stories or testimonials.
•	Employability skills taught:
•	Soft skills and professional development integrated into the curriculum.
7. Teaching and Learning
•	Faculty qualifications and expertise:
•	Information about teaching staff and their industry/research backgrounds.
•	Technology and resources:
•	Specific learning technologies, labs, or tools available to students.
•	Student workload and pacing:
•	Expected weekly study hours.
•	Flexibility for part-time or working students.
8. Student Support
•	Details on support services:
•	Specific academic support (tutoring, mentoring).
•	Wellbeing and mental health services.
•	Career services and internship placement assistance.
•	Support for international students.
9. Program Differentiators
•	Quantifiable evidence:
•	Metrics or data supporting claims of uniqueness.
•	Awards, accreditations, or recognitions received.
10. Pricing and Commercial Information
•	Pricing strategy and payment options:
•	Full fee schedule.
•	Installment plans or payment flexibility.
•	Revenue targets or enrollment goals:
•	Expected number of students per intake.
11. Legal and Compliance
•	Regulatory approvals or accreditations:
•	National or international accreditation status.
•	Compliance with education standards.
Summary
While the notes provide a strong conceptual overview and highlight program strengths, key practical and commercial details are missing for a full product brief. To complete the brief, the university will need to supply detailed curriculum information, market data, admissions criteria, pricing, faculty profiles, student support specifics, and more concrete evidence of program outcomes and competitive positioning.
Let me know if you'd like me to help draft follow-up questions or a template to collect this missing information!
